    <briefdescription>
<para>Compute the complementarity between two molecules. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>The score returned is infinity if the two molecules overlap by more than the value set for <ref refid="classIMP_1_1multifit_1_1ComplementarityRestraint_1af73ad08df0acb0c0d7fdbc415c4d79f1" kindref="member">set_maximum_penetration_score()</ref> or if the two molecules are separated by more than <ref refid="classIMP_1_1multifit_1_1ComplementarityRestraint_1a882c153d956b972f12180cf0af3a15c6" kindref="member">set_maximum_separation()</ref>.</para>
<para>Otherwise, the integral of the <ref refid="classIMP_1_1multifit_1_1ComplementarityRestraint_1ad5cf5dc29719b19a9deb7cb3504c6250" kindref="member">set_complementarity_value()</ref> value over a layer of thickness <ref refid="classIMP_1_1multifit_1_1ComplementarityRestraint_1a9f77d866de59965ead8ce1626ffa4d52" kindref="member">set_complementarity_thickness()</ref> around the molecule is returned.</para>
<para><simplesect kind="note"><para>currently each of the particle lists must belong to exactly one rigid body. And, currently, each rigid body can only be associated with one list of particles (that is, there cannot be multiple restraints with different lists of particles that all belong to the same rigid body).</para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="note"><para>The restraint does not support derivatives.</para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="note"><para>The restraint pretty much only makes sense for use with Domino as it returns infinity for many cases. Monte Carlo might make sense with certain parameters. </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
    </detaileddescription>
